{{also|MOODLE}}
==English==

===Etymology 1===
{{unk|en}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# To [[dawdle]] aimlessly, to idle time away.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1931| title=Music in London, 1890-94|author=Bernard Shaw | page=154| passage=Over a sonata by Schubert he — well, I suppose I must not say that he '''moodled''', or maundered, or anything of that sort.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2002| title=Passion and Pedagogy|author=Elijah Mirochnik, ‎Debora Claiborne Sherman | page=354| passage=The Poet '''moodles''' (from Brenda Ueland's If You Want to Write). She fools around, meanders, and takes time for feelings and not just ideas.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2006| title=Whatever Happened to Delight?|author=J. Barrie Shepherd | page=69| passage=I cannot begin to count the number of times I have '''moodled''', set a sermon aside, often in midstream, and taken a walk through the church gardens, or around the city block, even caught a brief nap on the study sofa; in other words, I have let my mind lie fallow, and then returned to the task with inspiration renewed.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2015| title=The Lost Track of Time |author=Paige Britt | page=62| passage=She '''moodled''' all day and all night. Hundreds of tiny notions streamed from her head and ideas bounced back and forth, but no real possibilities formed. }}

